/ref 17592186754773 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754434 1079 #uuid "031a2bfc-b0da-5a36-b1be-59804efe4622" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754435 1064 "**MTH 341 — Written Homework 5**\n\nDue: 2026-11-04\nDue time: 23:59:00 America/Los_Angeles (PST).\nDate imported: 2026-11-06\nSource: [MTH341-WHW5.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FWHW-5%2FMTH341-WHW5.pdf)\nDeadline source: [Canvas](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064363/assignments/10695098), verified 2026-11-06.\n\nQuestion source: [Kuttler, *Linear Algebra: A First Course*, 2017A](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F341%2FKuttler-LinearAlgebra-AFirstCourse-2017A.pdf). Exercise and printed-page numbers were checked against this edition; PDF page positions are recorded below.\n\nStudy answers, explanations, and graphics labeled “Authored reference” are additions, not publisher-provided solutions.\n\n## Instructions\n\nWrite solutions on separate letter-size (8.5 × 11 in.) or A4 paper and use the assignment sheet as the cover. Show all work and write neatly. Submit a single PDF through Canvas by the posted deadline; late homework is not accepted. Technology such as WolframAlpha may be used for row reduction unless an exercise says otherwise. The current Canvas assignment is worth 10 points: 8 for completeness and 2 for correctness of local Problem 2 (Kuttler 4.10.33). The old dates retained in the reused handout do not set the current deadline." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754435 1066 #uuid "0423cec1-8c19-59e6-aedb-2e433ad28ba7" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754435 1067 "Assignment information" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754436 1080 "No. They are the same function, and $W=(r_2-r_1)e^{(r_1+r_2)x}=0$ everywhere. They are dependent and cannot form a fundamental set of two solutions." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754436 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754436 1083 #uuid "04e43ee8-1045-5c40-aefe-d5e3fe209808" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754556 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754707 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754437 1071 "**(b)** For the same ODE, attempt $y_p=A_0x+A_1x^2+A_2x^3$. Does this work? Find its coefficients and explain your conclusion.\n\n$y_p(x)=$ {{particular}}. Give the coefficient equations and conclusion in the written response." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754437 1072 #uuid "064c2127-3fd0-5162-b6c5-0c2e390e82fe" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754437 1074 "Adding one power of $x$ lets the derivative trial retain an $x^2$ term. Match coefficients from the highest degree downward." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754438 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754681 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754438 1062 #uuid "082ff34d-391a-51fe-8bfa-6c9cfcf7f27e" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754438 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754782 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754439 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754792 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754439 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754439 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754439 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754792 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754439 1079 #uuid "0866243c-885c-5e3c-8679-491eae60fb28" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754440 1080 "undefined" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754440 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754440 1083 #uuid "09942cd0-6a8d-5cb4-9802-13a527603c69" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754484 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754441 1071 "**(b)** Show that $\\left(0,\\frac{4b}{3\\pi}\\right)$ is the centroid of $\\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\\frac{y^2}{b^2}\\le1$, $y\\ge0$, with $a,b>0$. Use the suggested change of variables $u=x/a$, $v=y/b$." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754441 1072 #uuid "0a29f5a1-52aa-5f76-a774-9acf7f6e7c39" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754441 1074 "Apply the stipulated scaling, keep its Jacobian in both denominator and numerator, and use the unit-semicircle moment." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754442 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754461 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754442 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754442 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754442 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754461 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754442 1079 #uuid "0aa33f27-0f84-515b-be13-a9f4c77a0ec5" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754443 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048813 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754443 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050150 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754443 1089 #edb/ref 17592186754741 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754443 1090 #uuid "0ada73b5-8c18-58e0-a2c5-f5a6a2f8f60d" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754444 1080 "Write $r^2=x^2+y^2+z^2$. Then $\\nabla T=-2e^{-r^2}\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$, so $$\\mathbf F=2e^{-r^2}\\langle x,y,z\\rangle.$$ It points radially outward, vanishes at the origin, and has magnitude $2re^{-r^2}$, increasing until $r=1/\\sqrt2$ and then decreasing. Draw arrows in all radial directions, with tails inside $r<2$.\n\n![Authored reference: outward heat-flow field inside the ball](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-6%2Fauthored-heat-flow-ball.png)" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754444 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754444 1083 #uuid "0b144580-ad09-5fc4-be67-0efee377fc38" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754445 1080 "-\\sin x-\\sin y" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754445 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754445 1083 #uuid "0bb4d8ae-8b67-5899-b4e4-fbf416906250" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754446 1080 "c_1+\\frac{c_2}{x}" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754446 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754446 1083 #uuid "0bc851c9-f173-58aa-a855-8c69f903f7e5" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754447 1080 "A nondegenerate local minimum has the usual source-like gradient picture: nearby gradient arrows point toward increasing values, away from the minimum. At any $C^2$ local minimum the Hessian is positive semidefinite, so $\\nabla^2f=f_{xx}+f_{yy}\\ge0$. Strictness alone does **not** imply $\\nabla^2f>0$: $f=x^4+y^4$ has a strict minimum at $(0,0)$ but Laplacian $0$ there. Thus strict minimum alone does not guarantee a source in the strict positive-divergence sense, nor radial outwardness of every nearby arrow." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754447 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754447 1083 #uuid "0d20b11c-f93d-53fd-8e50-540adc54e648" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754448 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754691 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754448 1062 #uuid "0e37398d-2085-5bd5-8191-e2f5b9494e50" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754448 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754815 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754598 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754449 1071 "Source question: Assignment item (6); Kuttler 2017A, Exercise 4.10.55, printed p. 230 (PDF p. 242).\n\nIf five vectors in $\\mathbb R^5$ are linearly independent, must they span $\\mathbb R^5$? Explain." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754449 1072 #uuid "0e506f02-0b2b-508e-acd7-212a12893031" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754449 1074 "Explain the link between a square matrix’s independent columns, full rank, and its column space being the whole ambient space." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754836 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754450 1071 "**(a)** Find the components of $\\mathbf F$. $\\mathbf F=$ {{answer}}." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754450 1072 #uuid "0e745f99-6980-578d-a22d-9dc8dba8d29e" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754450 1074 "Expanding $\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf r$ gives first component $bz-cy$, second component $cx-az$, and third component $ay-bx$." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754451 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754750 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754451 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754451 1077 "div-one" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754451 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754750 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754451 1079 #uuid "0ea89653-4621-5ab6-8c6a-943ff8d35cb2" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754644 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754452 1071 "**(a)** $T:\\ x=u+v,\\ y=u-v$. Find all four requested features." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754452 1072 #uuid "0ea9df61-12f4-512f-9777-33076fed78f0" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754452 1074 "Map the four corners in the original traversal order, fill the enclosed polygon, and compare the orientation with the determinant’s sign." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754453 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754459 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754453 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754453 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754453 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754459 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754453 1079 #uuid "0f937207-85a6-5a19-a7b1-1fa663c37cbf" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754454 1088 #edb/ref 17592186048130 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754454 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050537 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754454 1089 #edb/ref 17592186754457 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754454 1090 #uuid "0fa39910-5d4f-546d-9668-7fbb1e2a5ef0" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754739 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754455 1071 "**(b)** $\\nabla\\cdot\\phi$.\n\nClassification: {{answer}}." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754455 1072 #uuid "10392aca-57ae-5d69-8973-b99a29ba6d2c" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754455 1074 "Divergence requires a vector input; the supplied input is scalar." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754456 1080 "The region is symmetric about the $y$-axis, so $\\iint_Rx\\,dA=0$ and $x^*=0$. In polar coordinates $0\\le r\\le a$, $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$. Its area is $A=\\pi a^2/2$, while $$\\iint_Ry\\,dA=\\int_0^\\pi\\int_0^a r^2\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\frac{2a^3}{3}.$$ Hence $y^*=(2a^3/3)/(\\pi a^2/2)=4a/(3\\pi)$, as claimed." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754456 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754456 1083 #uuid "10617739-33b7-50ed-9e39-daa2aae3887d" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754457 1084 #edb/ref 17592186754633 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754457 1084 #edb/ref 17592186754810 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754457 1085 "Source question: PDF p. 4, Problem 3 (a)–(b)." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754457 1086 #uuid "10854064-5bc8-5e3b-b943-4668a08e3f48" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754457 1087 #edb/ref 17592186754810 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754458 1080 "3" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754458 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754458 1083 #uuid "11194f36-43d8-5076-a7c9-f7d87647842c" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754459 1080 "They are independent exactly when $r_1\\ne r_2$, because $W=(r_2-r_1)x^{r_1+r_2-1}$ is then nonzero on $x>0$. If $r_1=r_2$, the two functions coincide, their Wronskian is zero, and they are dependent. The question therefore requires this case split unless distinctness from part (b) is intended to carry over." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754459 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754459 1083 #uuid "1151b16b-d7a9-5847-926c-2f3ca6f45413" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754477 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754817 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754460 1071 "Source question: Assignment item (5); Kuttler 2017A, Exercise 4.10.47, printed p. 229 (PDF p. 241).\n\nConsider $$S=\\left\\{\\begin{pmatrix}4u+v-5w\\\\12u+6v-6w\\\\4u+4v+4w\\end{pmatrix}:u,v,w\\in\\mathbb R\\right\\}.$$ Is $S$ a subspace of $\\mathbb R^3$? If so, explain why, give a basis, and find its dimension.\n\nDimension (if a subspace): {{dimension}}. Give the subspace argument and a basis in the written response." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754460 1072 #uuid "12e75141-70ea-54e2-821a-9b304368f86c" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754460 1074 "Collect the coefficients of $u,v,w$ as generating vectors. Expressing the set as their span establishes the subspace claim before extracting a basis." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754461 1080 "The curl is $\\langle-1,0,0\\rangle$: its negative $x$-component corresponds to the clockwise wheel in (a), and its zero $y$- and $z$-components agree with no rotation in (b) and (c)." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754461 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754461 1083 #uuid "13189e39-0b95-509e-893f-1307327d809c" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754764 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754462 1071 "**(c)** If $r_1=r_2$, do $e^{r_1x},e^{r_2x}$ form a fundamental set? Explain using the Wronskian." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754462 1072 #uuid "1349237d-10f7-505a-843f-44bfbffaccb9" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754462 1074 "In this equal-root case dependence follows directly from equality of the two functions, as well as from their zero Wronskian." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754463 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754765 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754463 1062 #uuid "14365adc-8207-54f9-bb7e-26959dd05911" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754463 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754799 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754464 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754536 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754464 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754464 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754464 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754536 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754464 1079 #uuid "155ecdc4-8bfd-598e-acb8-bb7bf2a254aa" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754465 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754468 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754465 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754465 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754465 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754468 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754465 1079 #uuid "15931803-7295-5c5e-bca3-c7c66270f232" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754466 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754480 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754466 1062 #uuid "16990ab9-7a0a-5a08-9108-8c9301764bcf" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754466 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754809 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754467 1080 "By linearity of differentiation, $$\\begin{aligned}L[y_{p1}+y_{p2}]&=(y_{p1}\\prime\\prime+y_{p2}\\prime\\prime)+p(x)(y_{p1}\\prime+y_{p2}\\prime)+q(x)(y_{p1}+y_{p2})\\\\&=\\bigl(y_{p1}\\prime\\prime+p(x)y_{p1}\\prime+q(x)y_{p1}\\bigr)+\\bigl(y_{p2}\\prime\\prime+p(x)y_{p2}\\prime+q(x)y_{p2}\\bigr)\\\\&=f_1(x)+f_2(x).\\end{aligned}$$ Thus $y_{p1}+y_{p2}$ is a particular solution of the summed-forcing equation on the same interval." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754467 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754467 1083 #uuid "16c3c190-8757-5196-b278-20342077b975" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754468 1080 "The image boundary is $(0,0)\\to(1,1)\\to(0,2)\\to(-1,1)\\to(0,0)$, traversed counterclockwise. The filled region is $R=\\{(x,y):0\\le x+y\\le2,\\ 0\\le y-x\\le2\\}$. The Jacobian is $J=\\det\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}=2>0$, so orientation is preserved.\n\n![Authored reference: oriented image boundary](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-7%2Fauthored-boundary-b.png)" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754468 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754468 1083 #uuid "16e61cc7-ecdd-556d-8c99-669f948b1fec" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754818 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754469 1071 "**(b)** If $r_1\\ne r_2$, do $e^{r_1x},e^{r_2x}$ form a fundamental set for the given ODE? Explain using the Wronskian." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754469 1072 #uuid "16f063b6-3241-5ec0-8199-416ca1a7ee79" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754469 1074 "Use the assumed solution property together with a nonzero Wronskian; independence alone would not establish that they solve a particular ODE." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754470 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754776 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754470 1062 #uuid "173de582-7e56-55be-a7b2-4ba30f09481c" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754470 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754804 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754471 1080 "undefined" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754471 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754471 1083 #uuid "179979d5-5fae-53e5-be31-8ad50d422bd2" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754472 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754535 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754472 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754472 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754472 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754535 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754472 1079 #uuid "17da24c2-0ebb-58cb-9e0f-91a626f8d385" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754473 1080 "vector field" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754473 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754473 1083 #uuid "18d4e05c-817a-5b98-97c5-ebfe9566bbef" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754474 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754440 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754474 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754474 1077 "answer" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754474 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754440 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754474 1079 #uuid "19943a9c-75a6-546d-9c8e-a19bd49c28f4" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754475 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754600 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754475 1062 #uuid "19be6eb4-b791-5acc-a175-4258359a67ed" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754475 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754454 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754476 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754467 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754476 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754476 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754476 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754467 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754476 1079 #uuid "19cc8c0d-8525-52ef-b752-58cd84d00e1d" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754477 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754487 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754477 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754477 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754477 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754487 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754477 1079 #uuid "19f78c4d-65b3-577b-b30c-b9bb54f625f4" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754478 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754475 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754478 1062 #uuid "1a4bfbb1-674d-52af-8085-d3a26224047e" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754478 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754721 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754479 1080 "Following the source CCW order $(0,0)\\to(1,0)\\to(1,1)\\to(0,1)\\to(0,0)$, the image boundary is the polygon $(0,0)\\to(1,1)\\to(2,0)\\to(1,-1)\\to(0,0)$, traversed clockwise. Its filled region is $R=\\{(x,y):0\\le x+y\\le2,\\ 0\\le x-y\\le2\\}$. The Jacobian is $J=\\det\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}=-2<0$, so orientation reverses.\n\n![Authored reference: oriented image boundary](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-7%2Fauthored-boundary-a.png)" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754479 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754479 1083 #uuid "1af19ab3-f75c-5eb2-b305-5c8c215bfac6" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754480 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754534 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754480 1062 #uuid "1b1271b4-8656-559a-93a8-bb2c96b1f4eb" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754480 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754819 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754481 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754657 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754481 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754481 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754481 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754657 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754481 1079 #uuid "1c0b1511-4d9d-5803-86d8-a30bf6793c61" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754485 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754553 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754569 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754482 1071 "**(b)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle y,x^2\\rangle$.\n\n![Source plot for scalar-curl predictions](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FR-6%2Fcurl-field-b.png)\n\nFirst record both sign predictions and visual reasoning in the written response. Then compute:\n\n$\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F(1,1)=$ {{curl-one}}; $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F(-1,1)=$ {{curl-two}}." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754482 1072 #uuid "1c55e333-621b-5a7c-a029-9fc3f9d7e633" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754482 1074 "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, use scalar curl $Q_x-P_y$; positive curl corresponds to CCW rotation viewed from the positive $z$-axis." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754483 1080 "A nondegenerate local maximum has the usual sink-like gradient picture: gradient arrows point toward higher values near the maximum. At any $C^2$ local maximum the Hessian is negative semidefinite, so $\\nabla^2f\\le0$. The inequality need not be strict even for a strict maximum: $f=-x^4-y^4$ has a strict maximum at $(0,0)$ with Laplacian $0$. Strictness alone therefore does not guarantee a sink in the strict negative-divergence sense." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754483 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754483 1083 #uuid "1cfc0f52-a597-55d4-a7c8-9ab0310ad2a9" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754484 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754680 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754484 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754484 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754484 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754680 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754484 1079 #uuid "1dca5d19-76a0-5a78-9455-31630f891ba1" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754485 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754495 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754485 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754485 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754485 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754495 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754485 1079 #uuid "1e05eee7-770b-5f4f-adcd-b688980a4e0d" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754486 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754574 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754486 1062 #uuid "1e5063a4-e6f3-50c8-a6de-770560161f3a" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754486 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754797 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754487 1080 "Yes. Write $$S=\\operatorname{span}\\left\\{\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\12\\\\4\\end{pmatrix},\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\6\\\\4\\end{pmatrix},\\begin{pmatrix}-5\\\\-6\\\\4\\end{pmatrix}\\right\\}.$$ A span is closed under addition and scalar multiplication, hence is a subspace. The third generator equals $-2$ times the first plus $3$ times the second. The first two are not scalar multiples; they are independent. Thus a basis is $\\{(4,12,4)^T,(1,6,4)^T\\}$ and $\\dim S=2$. The generator-matrix RREF is $\\begin{pmatrix}1&0&-2\\\\0&1&3\\\\0&0&0\\end{pmatrix}$." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754487 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754487 1083 #uuid "1f4efacc-5f84-5c86-b3be-ec24795f9b82" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754488 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754694 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754488 1062 #uuid "1fae43aa-af2b-5953-89bb-e7fc4e37872c" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754488 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754688 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754489 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754805 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754489 1062 #uuid "20162cb9-268a-51ea-af7e-ab95af6c4d24" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754489 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754498 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754490 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754727 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754490 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754490 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754490 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754727 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754490 1079 #uuid "2100b8e7-7c20-5419-b9c4-e70668091dcf" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754491 1080 "no rotation" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754491 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754491 1083 #uuid "22ff67c5-27d3-558b-b182-68eb10da454c" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754492 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754816 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754492 1062 #uuid "2320565a-974e-5a63-90d0-b0c7cc3311b2" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754492 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754617 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754493 1075 #edb/ref 17592186754456 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754493 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408167 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754493 1077 "response" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754493 1078 #edb/ref 17592186754456 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754493 1079 #uuid "2338383e-8e3e-5521-8588-38b0a2c00d38" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754494 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754714 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754494 1062 #uuid "23b497b4-e2a5-5aeb-8edb-bcf70673262f" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754494 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754584 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754495 1080 "Predict positive/CCW rotation at $(1,1)$ and negative/CW rotation at $(-1,1)$. Since $Q_x-P_y=2x-1$, the values are $1$ and $-3$, respectively. The contribution from the vertica