omponent derivatives, factor out the positive exponential, then classify the sign of $6-4r^2$. Include the ball’s boundary when describing the full ball." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754583 1080 "undefined" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754583 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754583 1083 #uuid "5a6394c5-0ee0-5bc3-9406-9584d945dbef" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754808 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754584 1071 "**(i)** $\\nabla(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F)$.\n\nClassification: {{answer}}." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754584 1072 #uuid "5baeccef-fa87-5472-9b3c-49c05d496457" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754584 1074 "The inner divergence is scalar, so its gradient is a vector field." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754506 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754585 1071 "Source question: PDF p. 3, Problem 3.\n\nThe gravitational potential energy $y(x)$ of an orbiting object at distance $x$ from the center of a large spherically symmetric body obeys $x^2y\\prime\\prime+2xy\\prime=0$. Find the general solution (the possible potential-energy functions) on $x>0$, using arbitrary constants $c_1,c_2$.\n\n$y(x)=$ {{answer}}." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754585 1072 #uuid "5bfc20a9-7fc2-5580-9489-9622c62e34b5" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754585 1074 "Try $y=x^r$. The indicial equation is $r(r-1)+2r=r(r+1)=0$, with roots $0,-1$. Therefore $y=c_1+c_2/x$ on $x>0$. Independently, writing $v=y\\prime$ yields $x^2v\\prime+2xv=0$, so $v=K/x^2$; integrating yields the same two-constant family." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754671 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754586 1071 "**(f)** $\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F$.\n\nClassification: {{answer}}." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754586 1072 #uuid "5c21b2f3-aaa0-5d94-ac09-067f1b143bc8" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754586 1074 "The three-dimensional curl produces a vector field." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754675 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754587 1071 "**(b)** Find $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F$. $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F=$ {{answer}}." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754587 1072 #uuid "5f84ca98-c210-5ad5-9450-c57a24f79ae4" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754587 1074 "For $\\mathbf F=\\langle bz-cy,cx-az,ay-bx\\rangle$, the derivatives in the divergence are $\\partial_x(bz-cy)=0$, $\\partial_y(cx-az)=0$, and $\\partial_z(ay-bx)=0$. Their sum is zero." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754588 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049459 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754588 1088 #edb/ref 17592186049696 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754588 1088 #edb/ref 17592186050143 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754588 1088 #edb/ref 17592186051085 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754588 1089 #edb/ref 17592186754551 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754588 1090 #uuid "5fdc05b4-eb32-5b46-9839-127bc376b571" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754589 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754550 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754589 1062 #uuid "6165874e-661c-54c4-a330-ae9af95206c8" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754589 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754618 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754590 1080 "The assertion is true. Put $h=f-g$. Fix $(x_0,y_0)$. For fixed $y$, the one-variable derivative $\\partial_xh$ is zero, so the mean value theorem gives $h(x,y)=h(x_0,y)$. With $x=x_0$ fixed, $\\partial_yh=0$ gives $h(x_0,y)=h(x_0,y_0)$. Therefore $h$ is the constant $h(x_0,y_0)$ throughout $\\mathbb R^2$, hence $f=g+c$." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754590 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754590 1083 #uuid "6192f0fe-c382-5c21-81db-e5af8a58a07a" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754591 1080 "-3" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754591 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754591 1083 #uuid "625c4776-ee83-5b8d-8e5f-d7005eda8c28" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754592 1058 #edb/ref 17592186754579 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754592 1062 #uuid "6264d1d2-f04a-5bb1-852c-d3ac66215518" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754592 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754752 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186754747 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754593 1071 "**(a)** $\\nabla\\phi$.\n\nClassification: {{answer}}." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754593 1072 #uuid "63e5e343-ff11-5816-9cbc-1bf7cc665f4a" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754593 1074 "The gradient takes a scalar function to a vector field." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754594 1062 #uuid "64e19c92-08e0-526c-8adf-51b0d8c4bd48" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754594 1063 #edb/ref 17592186754673 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754595 1080 "This trial has $y_p\\prime=A_1+2A_2x$ and $y_p\\prime\\prime=2A_2$, hence $$y_p\\prime\\prime+y_p\\prime=(A_1+2A_2)+2A_2x.$$ Its $x^2$ coefficient is zero, whereas the forcing has $x^2$ coefficient $1$. Matching coefficients would require $0=1$, so no choice of these constants works. The constant term of the trial is annihilated by $D^2+D$." 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754595 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754595 1083 #uuid "66b4c474-16aa-5db7-bcce-7a378f52f74e" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754596 1080 "\\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\\\\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}" 13194139546561 17592186502323 true] [17592186754596 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139546561 17592186502323 true]